      <div class="research-entry-content">
        <h3>
          <a href="/projects/knowledge-informed-ece">
            Knowledge-Informed Constrained Learning for Data-Efficient
            Extracapsular Extension Assessment in Head and Neck CT
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p class="research-entry-authors">
          <strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, William Neil Duggar, and
          Haifeng Wang
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-status">
          Under review at <em>Medical Image Analysis</em>
        </p>

        <p
          class="research-entry-links"
          aria-label="Knowledge-Informed ECE resources"
        >
          <a
            href="https://github.com/amirhossein-eskorouchi/Knowledge-Informed-ECE"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Code
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a
            href="https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Amirhossein-Eskorouchi-2/publication/392593672_Knowledge-Informed_Automated_Extracapsular_Extension_Detection_in_Head_and_Neck_Cancer/links/689bede6daa95834904ecd69/Knowledge-Informed-Automated-Extracapsular-Extension-Detection-in-Head-and-Neck-Cancer.pdf"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Conference paper
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a href="/projects/knowledge-informed-ece">Project page</a>
        </p>

        <p class="publication-release-note">
          <strong>Note:</strong>
          The repository is available; complete code and experimental results
          will be added after the corresponding manuscript is published.
        </p>
        <div class="research-entry-details">
          <p>
            <strong>Description:</strong>{" "}
            Extracapsular extension, the spread of cancer beyond a lymph-node
            capsule, is a major prognostic factor in head and neck cancer, but
            its preoperative identification on CT is difficult because the
            imaging signs are subtle and pathology-confirmed labels are scarce.
            Rather than learning from the entire scan or using segmentation
            only as preprocessing, this framework embeds lymph-node anatomy
            directly into both feature learning and decision-making. A
            dual-output 3D SwinUNETR jointly localizes nodal and perinodal
            regions and classifies ECE, while the predicted mask forms soft
            spatial weights that determine which latent features reach the
            classifier. Clinically informed mask expansion models disease
            spread beyond the nodal boundary, and Mask-in-Mask pretraining on
            approximately 1,500 unlabeled CT volumes improves learning under
            limited supervision.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Outcome:</strong>{" "}
            On 150 patients with pathology-confirmed ECE status, the proposed
            model achieved an accuracy of 0.924, an AUC of 0.928, a sensitivity
            of 0.926, and a segmentation Dice score of 0.821. It achieved the
            strongest accuracy, AUC, sensitivity, and Dice among the evaluated
            methods. At inference, the model predicts its own anatomical region
            and uses that prediction to guide classification, preserving a
            fully automatic pathway while grounding decisions in clinically
            meaningful evidence.
          </p>

          <p class="research-entry-support">
            <strong>Project support:</strong>{" "}NIH-USA
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

