      <div class="research-entry-content">
        <h3>
          <a href="/projects/ml-psg-adhd">
            Machine Learning-Based Polysomnography Data Analysis for ADHD
            Diagnosis: A Focus on Sleep Stage-Based Biomarkers
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p class="research-entry-authors">
          <strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, Haifeng Wang,
          Jonathan W. Lee, Vignesh H. Nayak, Norma B. Ojeda, and Lir-Wan Fan
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-status">
          <em>
            Proceedings of the 2025 IEEE 22nd International Symposium on
            Biomedical Imaging (ISBI 2025)
          </em>
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-links" aria-label="ML-PSG-ADHD resources">
          <a
            href="https://github.com/amirhossein-eskorouchi/ML-PSG-ADHD-Analysis"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Code
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.1109/ISBI60581.2025.10981031"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Paper
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a href="/projects/ml-psg-adhd">Project page</a>
        </p>

        <div class="research-entry-details">
          <p>
            <strong>Description:</strong>{" "}
            This study investigates sleep-stage-specific physiological
            connectivity as an objective source of ADHD biomarkers. Pearson
            correlations across 17 EEG, EOG, EMG, and ECG channels are
            converted into weighted graphs for each participant and sleep
            stage. Average shortest-path length summarizes each graph, forming
            a five-dimensional participant representation spanning Wake,
            Sleep Stages 1, 2, and 3-4, and rapid eye movement sleep. A Random
            Forest classifier then evaluates these graph-derived features
            through nested cross-validation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Outcome:</strong>{" "}
            The published study analyzed 48 participants and more than 40,000
            30-second PSG epochs. The model achieved average accuracy of 72%,
            precision of 71%, recall of 85%, and an F1 score of 76%. Feature
            importance and permutation analysis identified Sleep Stage 1 and
            Sleep Stage 3-4 as the most influential features for
            distinguishing ADHD and control participants.
          </p>

          <p class="research-entry-support">
            <strong>Project support:</strong>{" "}
            System Intelligence and Optimization Lab
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

