      <div class="research-entry-content">
        <h3>
          <a href="/projects/pdarts-skin-transferability">
            Understanding Knowledge Transferability in Differentiable Architecture Search for Skin Lesion Diagnosis
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p class="research-entry-authors">
          Anh Vo, Lu He,{" "}<strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, and Haifeng Wang
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-venue">
          <em>IISE Transactions on Healthcare Systems Engineering</em>,
          16(1), 71-90, 2026
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-links">
          <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.1080/24725579.2026.2622416"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Article
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/amirhossein-eskorouchi/pdarts-skin-transferability"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Code
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
          <a href="/projects/pdarts-skin-transferability">
            Project page
          </a>
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Description:</strong>
          This study investigates whether neural architectures discovered
          through Progressive Differentiable Architecture Search on one
          skin-lesion dataset remain effective under different acquisition
          conditions, class distributions, and data scales. The architecture
          is transferred from dermoscopic ISIC-2019 images to PAD-UFES-20
          smartphone images and DermaMNIST, while all target-task weights are
          initialized and trained anew.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Outcome:</strong>
          The experiments show that transferable P-DARTS architectures remain
          competitive across datasets, resolutions, and diagnostic
          formulations. At 224&times;224 resolution, P-DARTS6 reached mean
          accuracies of 69.06% on PAD-UFES-20 and 79.48% on DermaMNIST, while
          the best reported binary PAD-UFES-20 run achieved 97.71%. The
          analysis also found that evaluation-network depth mattered more than
          the search-to-evaluation depth gap and that pooling operations were
          associated with transfer behavior.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
